<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="dTDP-L-rhamnose biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="DTDPRHAMSYN-PWY" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: CPD0-1112| is a deoxysugar that functions as a building block of the glycan component of the lipopolysaccharide present in ECA, the enterobacterial common antigen, and of the O-antigens of many bacterial species |CITS: [11114506][14670712]|. |FRAME: L-rhamnose| is also a component of the plant cell wall pectic polysaccharides |FRAME: Rhamnogalacturonans-I &quot;rhamnogalacturonan type I&quot;| and |FRAME: CPD-14581 &quot;rhamnogalacturonan type II&quot;| and is present in diverse plant secondary metabolites including anthocyanins, flavonoids and triterpenoids. |FRAME: L-rhamnose| has also been observed in archaebacteria. For example, it is the terminal saccharide of the tetrasaccharide attached to the surface (S)-layer glycoprotein Asn-498 of the halophilic archaeon |FRAME: TAX-2246| |CITS: [22029420][24194539][24831810]|. Some bacteria such as the actinomycete |FRAME: TAX-60894| also utilize |FRAME: CPD0-1112| as a component of secondary metabolites (see |FRAME: PWY-7100| for an example) |CITS: [11544225 ]| |CITS: [19126547]|. Like most deoxysugars, |FRAME: CPD0-1112| is synthesized in the activated form of a nucleotide sugar - |FRAME: DTDP-RHAMNOSE|. However, at least in some plants, such as mung bean (|FRAME: TAX-157791|), both |FRAME: DTDP-RHAMNOSE| and |FRAME: UDP-L-RHAMNOSE| act as sugar donors for the rhamnosylation of flavonoids |CITS: [13864832 ]|. Biosynthesis starts with |FRAME: GLC-1-P|, which is activated by |FRAME: TTP| and converted to |FRAME: DTDP-DEOH-DEOXY-GLUCOSE| in two steps. The first enzyme of the pathway, RmlA (RfbA) (|FRAME: EC-2.7.7.24 &quot;EC 2.7.7.24&quot;|), catalyzes the transfer of a thymidylmonophosphate nucleotide to |FRAME: GLC-1-P| |CITS: [11053865]|. The second enzyme, RmlB (|FRAME: EC-4.2.1.46 &quot;EC 4.2.1.46&quot;|), catalyzes the oxidation of the C4 hydroxyl group of the sugar, followed by dehydration |CITS: [10666612][11243820]|. These two steps are common in the synthesis of many modified sugars used for O-antigen synthesis (see |FRAME: PWY-7317|). |FRAME: DTDP-DEOH-DEOXY-GLUCOSE| is then converted to |FRAME:DTDP-DEOH-DEOXY-MANNOSE| by the action of RmlC (|FRAME: EC-5.1.3.13 &quot;EC 5.1.3.13&quot;|) |CITS: [10089454][10802738]|. In most organisms this enzyme only participates in |FRAME: CPD0-1112| production, although in some streptomyces species it also participates in synthesis of |FRAME: CPD-15496| (see |FRAME: PWY-7301|) |CITS: [15752721]|. The final enzyme of this pathway, |FRAME: EC-1.1.1.133|, reduces the carbon at the 4th position, resulting in |FRAME: CPD0-1112| |CITS: [10666586]|. The pathway is ubiquitous and highly conserved in both Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. It is also found in plants, fungi, and lower eukaryotes |CITS: [27009306]|. The last two enzymatic activities (epimerase and reductase) are performed in plants by one bifunctional enzyme, NRS/ER |CITS: [15020741]|.</Description>
